Subject: windows.gaming.input: GIP providers for Xbox controllers, with every message to the controller blocked

On Windows, Xbox controllers attached by USB are GIP devices and apps' factories registered for a GIP interface
get them as GipGameControllerProviders. Wine sees them as HID gamepads, so a Microsoft controller that is a GIP
device over USB (Xbox One, One S, Elite, Elite 2, Adaptive, Series X|S) is now also wrapped in a GIP provider
declaring the interfaces every gamepad declares ([MS-GIPUSB]: IController, IGamepad, INavigationController), and
given to the factories registered for them: when it arrives, and when a factory registers after it did. Custom
controllers are the app's own objects (only Wine's implement IGameControllerImpl).

Nothing is sent to the controller. GipGameControllerProvider.SendMessage and SendReceiveMessage log the message
and fail with E_ACCESSDENIED, UpdateFirmwareAsync fails at once, and LegacyGipGameControllerProvider (now given for
these controllers) refuses SetHomeLedIntensity, SetStandardControllerButtonRemapping, ExecuteCommand, the headset
settings and pilot/copilot pairing. Its reads answer what is known: the interfaces, a real and uncorrupted
device, and an unknown battery (the host's driver doesn't report it).

Also: providers' FirmwareVersionInfo (the HID version number, the USB bcdDevice in BCD, e.g. 5.17), an unknown
HardwareVersionInfo, IsConnected, and NonRoamableId (the device path) were E_NOTIMPL. A GIP device's
GameControllerProviderInfo.GetProviderId is a 64-bit id in hex, as apps parse it: a hash of the device id.
TryGetFactoryControllerFromGameController matches by device.

With these, Xbox Accessories lists an Xbox Series controller attached by USB.

